-#: at all.
+#: storey above the pavement — not what the repeat has to look like.
 #:
 #: So the floor is enforced on what it is about. ``solve_structure`` asserts
 #: it over the ``bay`` slots the solver returns — the postcondition matching
 #: the ``_BAY_PITCH_MAX`` tripwire on the entrance, so that the band is now
 #: guarded on both sides, each over its own domain — and the entrance carries
 #: its own two-sided guard there against the bounds that are genuinely its.
+#:
+#: **A24 — the figure was 2.70 m and the corpus refutes it.** The paragraph
+#: that used to close this note argued that "a facade built of 2.35 m bays is
+#: not the same claim at all". 77 bd de Sébastopol IS a facade built of 2.35 m
+#: bays: six openings across a 14.75 m frontage, measured off the plate at
+#: 57.6 px/m and agreeing with Daly's own stated pitch of 2.353 to a
+#: centimetre. So is 1 bd de Sébastopol at 2.24, and 9 rue du Conservatoire at
+#: 2.09. **Six of the sixteen sit under 2.70**, and two of them are the
+#: commercial Sébastopol frontages this typology is calibrated against.
+#:
+#: A floor that excludes 37 % of the corpus is not a floor, it is a preference
+#: — and its cost was paid in the pier. With the window ABSOLUTE and the bay
+#: elastic, a floor too high forces too FEW bays and every surplus centimetre
+#: of frontage goes into masonry: measured, our repeating pitch came out 3.03 m
+#: against 2.24-3.02 across four plates, and our pier 1.83 m against 1.21-1.93.
+#: Reported by the operator as the gap between windows being too wide, which is
+#: exactly what it was.
+#:
+#: SOURCED now, at the narrowest pitch Daly draws.
+#: **A24 — the pier is bounded against the window, and the bay follows.**
+#:
+#: The operator's rule, from reading the plates: where nothing stands on the
+#: pier, it is never wider than 1.5x the window and never narrower than 0.8x.
+#: A bay is centreline to centreline, so it is one window plus one pier, and
+#: those two ratios ARE the bay's band — 1.8x to 2.5x the window.
+#:
+#: This is why the band is now COMPOSED from ``opening_window`` rather than
+#: stated as two free numbers. ``standard_bay`` admits in its own docstring
+#: that its bounds are "chosen so minimum/maximum bracket" the window and pier
+#: "without double-scaling (the composed bay is its own single Prop figure, not
+#: the arithmetic sum)". That independence is the defect: with the window
+#: ABSOLUTE and the bay elastic and unrelated to it, every centimetre of
+#: frontage the bay count declines to absorb goes into masonry, and nothing
+#: notices. Measured against four Daly plates our pier came out 1.83 m against
+#: their 1.21-1.93 and our pitch 3.03 m against their 2.24-3.02.
+_PIER_RATIO_MIN = 0.8
+_PIER_RATIO_MAX = 1.5
+
 _BAY_PITCH_MIN = Abs(
-    2.70, "the narrowest REPEATING bay a window and its two piers make sense in"
+    round(float(catalogue.opening_window().preferred) * (1.0 + _PIER_RATIO_MIN), 3),
+    f"one window plus a pier at {_PIER_RATIO_MIN:g}x its width — the narrowest "
+    "the pier may be before it stops reading as masonry carrying a lintel (A24)",
 )
 
 #: ABSOLUTE. The widest bay: what a stone lintel spans over an opening, and
 #: what the floor joists behind it run, before the wall wants a column.
 _BAY_PITCH_MAX = Abs(
-    3.40, "the widest bay a stone lintel and the joist run behind it will span"
+    round(float(catalogue.opening_window().preferred) * (1.0 + _PIER_RATIO_MAX), 3),
+    f"one window plus a pier at {_PIER_RATIO_MAX:g}x its width — past this the "
+    "wall wants something standing on it, and the rhythm should gain a bay "
+    "instead (A24)",
+)
+
+#: ABSOLUTE. What a stone lintel and the joists behind it will span — the bound
+#: on a FIXED slot, which is a different question from what the repeat may be.
+#:
+#: **A24, and it is A10.1's argument finished on the other side.** A10.1 already
+#: established that ``_BAY_PITCH_MIN`` is a floor under a *rhythm*, and that the
+#: entrance is "a one-off standing in that rhythm rather than a member of it" —
+#: which is why a 2.35 m *porte piétonne* is allowed to sit under it. The
+#: ceiling had no such partner: it was enforced against the entrance as though
+#: the entrance were a bay.
+#:
+#: That only went unnoticed while the repeat's ceiling happened to be 3.40 m —
+#: the same figure. Once A24 derived the repeat's band from the window and the
+#: pier ratio, the two separated, and a *porte cochère* at 3.40 m tripped a
+#: guard about rhythm using a number that was never about rhythm.
+#:
+#: They are genuinely different bounds. What limits a repeating bay is when the
+#: pier stops reading as masonry carrying a lintel. What limits a fixed slot is
+#: what the lintel over it can actually span. Daly's own buildings show the
+#: separation: 39 rue Neuve des Mathurins carries a carriage entrance wider than
+#: its own repeating bays, measured at 3.02 m pitch on the plate.
+_FIXED_SLOT_SPAN_MAX = Abs(
+    3.40, "the widest a stone lintel and the joist run behind it will span"
 )
 
 #: The pitch a facade takes when nothing forces it off, stated explicitly
@@ -2731,14 +2795,14 @@ def solve_structure(site: SiteContext, datums: BlockDatums | None = None) -> Gri
     # two modules agreeing, which is why it is checked instead of trusted:
